 soldiers led to a resurgence of swing music, as it became synonymous with American patriotism and morale."}, {"sourceSlug": "chicago", "sourceName": "Chicago, United States", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "swing", "targetName": "Swing", "context": "Swing music began to gain traction in Chicago during the 1920s, serving as a critical incubator for its development as a popular genre."}], "places": [{"name": "New York City, United States", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "Chicago, United States", "role": "Birthplace of Swing"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, United States", "role": "Major Venue"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: swing", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "swing"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q203775", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swing_music"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "swing contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Swing music significantly impacted American music culture, leading to the popularization of jazz and influencing various musical genres, ultimately shaping social dynamics during the 20th century.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:21Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Style of jazz or musical genre based on the rhythmic pulse of music composed of pairs of eighth notes with a longer initial note and a shorter second note Created c. 1930.", "newValue": "Swing is a vital style of jazz music that emerged in the early 1930s, fundamentally altering the landscape of American music and culture. Characterized by its distinctive rhythmic feel, swing is based on the interplay of pairs of eighth notes with a longer initial note followed by a shorter second note, creating a propulsive groove that invites both dancing and improvisation. The genre flourished primarily in the United States, with significant contributions from renowned musicians such as Duke Ellington, Count Basie, and Benny Goodman, who popularized swing through their orchestras and performances during the Great Depression.

The swing era, often attributed to the period between 1935 and 1945, saw the rise of big bands and a shift in jazz from small combo formats to larger ensembles. This transition was marked by the introduction of sophisticated arrangements, which incorporated elements of blues, rhythm and blues, and even classical music. Events such as the 1938 Carnegie Hall concert by Benny Goodman and the establishment of swing dance venues across the country elevated swing music's status. This era not only transformed jazz but also influenced popular music, paving the way for subsequent genres like rhythm and blues and rock and roll.

The legacy of swing is profound, influencing countless musicians and genres beyond its immediate timeline. The improvisational nature and rhythmic complexity of swing have inspired generations of jazz musicians, while also permeating popular music through artists ranging from Elvis Presley to modern-day hip-hop producers. Swing music also played a crucial role in the integration of American society, as it brought together diverse racial communities in both performance and dance, exemplifying the cultural melting pot that is America."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:21Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The Great Migration brought African American musicians to urban centers like Chicago and New York, fostering the development of jazz styles like swing.", "The economic hardships of the Great Depression led to a demand for music that provided joy and escapism, driving the popularity of swing music.", "Technological advancements in radio and recording allowed swing music to reach wider audiences, solidifying its place in American culture."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:21Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["Swing music catalyzed the evolution of jazz into a mainstream genre, influencing the development of future musical styles such as rock and roll.", "The popularity of swing led to the establishment of dance clubs and institutions that celebrated this music, influencing social culture and nightlife.", "Swing's integration of diverse musical influences contributed to the broader cultural exchange and racial integration in American society during the 20th century."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:21Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sou